{"verify_memory_ids":["memory_73","memory_86"],"intended_action":"simplify_onboarding","scale":"standard_experiment","uses_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31"],"downstream_value_risk_flagged":true,"preserves_uncertainty":true,"rationale":"The competitor's entry pricing makes promotional_pricing the emotionally obvious move, and memory_73 appears to endorse it (+31% signups, +18% revenue). But that record is exactly the kind I should distrust before betting the single slot on it: signup lift far exceeds revenue lift (ARPU dilution), the gains are concentrated in price-sensitive cohorts (the segment least likely to produce durable qualified revenue), it pools 12 weekly cohorts (masking decay or cohort heterogeneity), and it held acquisition channels fixed — an assumption that is now false, since a competitor is actively reshaping the price-sensitive channel. Discounting into a price war against a cheaper entrant also risks resetting our price anchor permanently. So memory_73 is my first verification: not because I plan to act on it, but because it is the highest-consequence claim pulling against my intent, and if the archive shows durable margin-positive retention I should reconsider.

My intended action instead targets the measured regressions. Onboarding completion is down 4% and activation down 6% QoQ, and these are plausibly causally linked — onboarding failure upstream of activation failure. memory_86 (shortened onboarding lifted completion and time-to-value) and memory_31 (signup field reduction produced durable SMB first-week activation lift) corroborate each other from independent sessions, and memory_31 explicitly reports durability, which is the property I care about. Fixing onboarding also indirectly defends against the competitor: better time-to-value is a retention moat that does not erode price. I verify memory_86 as my second pull because it is the memory I am actually acting on and it is the older, less specific of the pair — I want the original effect size, the population, and whether the lift persisted past week one.

Caveats I am not resolving: memory_86 and memory_31 both predate the current competitive environment and the QoQ declines, so the declines may have an external cause (competitor siphoning the very SMB accounts these flows serve) that no onboarding change will fix. Activation lift is also not the same as qualified revenue — memory_91 shows engagement can be nudged without evidence it converts, so I am flagging downstream value risk and will instrument this on retained qualified revenue, not completion rate, to avoid optimizing a proxy. Standard experiment scale rather than broad rollout, since two of my three supporting premises are unverified until the archive returns.","confidence":66}
